#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083126 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083324 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "179e0482-8d67-5e28-af2d-0d3aa7f0b770" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Given a Normal Distribution Model"}] :question/id #uuid "cdd74ee4-b82f-520f-8541-f328f9e63fd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-8279" :question/problem "The birth weights of newborns in a given population are normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,543$ grams and a standard deviation of $609$ grams. What is the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2643 \\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2090 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2061 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2033 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2358 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2327 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2709 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2676 \\,$ | $\\, 0.2643 \\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ |\n| $\\, -0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4286 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4247 \\,$ | $\\, 0.4207 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5319 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5359 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5398 \\,$ |\n| $\\, \\phantom{-}0.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5714 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5753 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5793 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the weight of a randomly selected baby. Then we have $X \\sim N(3\\,543, 609^2).$\n\nWe want to find $P(3\\,100 <   X <  3\\,600).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3\\,100<X<3\\,600) & =P(\\frac{3\\,100-3\\,543}{609}<Z<\\frac{3\\,600-3\\,543}{609}) \\\\  & =P(-0.73<Z<0.09)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $\\Phi(z)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.73<Z<0.09) & =P(Z<0.09)-P(Z<-0.73) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.09)-P(Z\\le -0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.09)-Φ(-0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-0.73) & =0.2327 \\\\ Φ(0.09) & =0.5359\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.73<Z<0.09) & =Φ(0.09)-Φ(-0.73) \\\\  & =0.5359-0.2327 \\\\  & =0.3032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the probability that a randomly selected newborn baby weighs more than $3\\,100$ grams but less than $3\\,600$ is $0.30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083127 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083325 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f06fdfdc-bba9-5bc6-8e32-722ec21da7d6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Population Parameter Using a Percentage Points Table"}] :question/id #uuid "5905ffa9-e7e2-5288-8714-91357fb17139" :question/math-academy-id "e-8280" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the episodes of a popular podcast is $43\\,\\textrm{min}.$ Additionally, $20\\%$ of the episodes have a duration of less than $38$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of a randomly selected episode is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the episode durations, rounded to the nearest minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.10 \\,$ | $\\, 0.15 \\,$ | $\\, 0.20 \\,$ | $\\, 0.25 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, -2.3263 \\,$ | $\\, -1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, -1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, -1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, -1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, -0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, -0.6745 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly selected episode. Then, we have $X \\sim N(43, \\sigma^2),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma.$ \n\nWe know that $P(X \\leq 38) = 0.20.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 38) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{38-43}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{5}{\\sigma }) & =0.20 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.20) =-0.8416.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =-0.8416 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1683 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.1683} \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈6\\,\\text{minutes.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083326 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "68479ded-5f29-5fd9-9604-e25bef7d5e32" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Probability When a Population Parameter is Unknown"}] :question/id #uuid "4049eb36-a8b0-5ef7-bf82-6f38cbe5cb17" :question/math-academy-id "e-8281" :question/problem "The systolic blood pressure of a group of people is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and a standard deviation of $12\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$ Additionally, $50\\%$ of the people have a systolic blood pressure between $0.93\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$ and $1.07\\mu\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}$. Find, as a percentage, the proportion of people with a systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p = \\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\, 0.75 \\,$ | $\\, 0.80 \\,$ | $\\, 0.85 \\,$ | $\\, 0.90 \\,$ | $\\, 0.95 \\,$ | $\\, 0.97 \\,$ | $\\, 0.99 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\Phi^{-1}(p)$ | $\\, 0.6745 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8416 \\,$ | $\\, 1.0364 \\,$ | $\\, 1.2816 \\,$ | $\\, 1.6449 \\,$ | $\\, 1.8808 \\,$ | $\\, 2.3263 \\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p = \\Phi(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the systolic blood pressure of a randomly selected person. Then we have $X \\sim N(\\mu, 12^2),$ and we want to find $P(X <  120).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu.$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.93\\mu  <   X <   1.07\\mu ) = 0.5.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.93\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.25 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.5+0.25 \\\\ P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.07\\mu ) & =0.75 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12}) & =0.75 \\\\ \\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.75).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n\\Phi^{-1}(0.75) \\approx 0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.07\\mu }{12} & =0.6745 \\\\ 0.07\\mu  & =8.094 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.094}{0.07} \\\\  & ≈115.629.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can find $P(X <   120)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<120) & =P(Z<\\frac{120-115.629}{12}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.36) \\\\  & =Φ(0.36) \\\\  & =0.6406 \\\\  & ≈64.1%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $64.1\\%$ of the people have systolic blood pressure lower than $120\\,\\text{mm}\\,\\text{Hg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083145 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083342 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/id #uuid "c55db5a3-2d8c-57d7-b860-8628cb8738f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9892" :question/problem "Which of the following data sets **might** be normally distributed?\n\n![](images/d4/d40721092d0e11fdc2ac582688491a922bd0c6240e5bfa44b6374a73f6577685.png)" :question/worked-solution "If a large data set is normally distributed, it should be approximately bell-shaped. \n\nAmong the given options, the only bell-shaped curve is shown in diagram II:\n\n![](images/38/382e3bc616bc15ac99fb5f18f1f9a75925eb2b5760152c7088fdbc7047fff904.png)\n\nIt's important to note that other bell-shaped distributions exist. Therefore, further tests on the data are required to confirm whether the underlying distribution is normal."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083528 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083342 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f79d9ff-425b-5f2e-8380-23435a01a5b4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Data Sets That Could Be Normally Distributed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fa31037-44ef-5261-8775-57dea5d4b13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/47d0812e7cb8b459df66a9d8f153232d10b5cd4532da4787dab0629b7ac0a1a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080064} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2db0d3-e7ef-5209-ae1a-a5840baaf6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/